Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-23
Subject: Use an explicit aword temporary in rautils val instead of casting the result var in place
License: expat

`rautils.pas` parses an integer literal by trying signed `val(...,
result, code)`, then on failure trying unsigned via `val(prefix+s,
aword(result), code)`. The second call casts the `var` parameter
target itself: `aword(result)` reinterprets the storage of an `int64`
result variable as if it were an `aword` for the duration of the call.

That is UB-adjacent at best. A reinterpret cast does not produce a
stable lvalue you can bind a `var` parameter to: the callee writes
through a reference whose declared type is `aword` while the storage's
true type is `int64`. Native FPC happens to compile it because both
fit in 8 bytes and FPC pokes the bytes directly, but lowered literally
to C++ it would need a reference to a `reinterpret_cast` result, which
the language does not let you take and which would alias UB-ly even if
it did. Use a plain `uresult: aword` temporary, parse into it, then
assign back. Same outcome, no reference to a reinterpret.

--- a/compiler/rautils.pas
+++ b/compiler/rautils.pas
@@ -572,6 +572,7 @@
   code : integer;
   errmsg : word;
   prefix : string[2];
+  uresult : aword;
 Begin
   case base of
     2 :
@@ -600,12 +601,13 @@
   val(prefix+s,result,code);
   if code<>0 then
     begin
-      val(prefix+s,aword(result),code);
+      val(prefix+s,uresult,code);
       if code<>0 then
         begin
           Message1(errmsg,s);
           result:=0;
         end;
+      result:=uresult;
     end;
 end;

